Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Offset: a rounding whose sides are chains of edges vanishes beside a
removed face, and a cap section split at a seam is joined (mitcad#2494).

Where the wall is thicker than a rounding beside a removed face (a cap),
FindVanishingFaces (patch 0100, 2433) took faces of exactly four edges
only. A rounding whose boundary other faces cut into many edges (GitHub
#645's loft with its top rounded 2 mm, shelled 3 mm with the top open: one
rounding of 47 edges and a sliver of 13 between two others) was kept, its
offset turned inside out and the offsets did not close. Now a face of more
edges whose boundary turns sharply (more than 30 degrees) at four vertices
only has four sides, the chains of edges between those corners
(FourSides); opposite sides are tested station by station along each of
their edges (VanishesBetweenSides), and the pairs across are given edge by
edge. Across the cap, every edge of a kept face to the vanishing faces
gets the face's section with the cap as its image (ContextIntByInt), the
walk round the face's wire counts consecutive edges of one image once
(Inter2d ConnexIntByInt), and a smooth edge ending beside a vanishing face
that touches a cap is extended to that section instead of being cut within
its offset (the walls of a slot meet tangentially). BRepAlgo_Loop no longer
merges two vertices of the face's own boundary, however near: a cap edge
shorter than the tolerance was closed on one vertex in place, and the kept
faces sharing it got open wires.

In the arc join, ExtentFace takes of a section of a face extended to a cap
that comes in a run of pieces sharing their ends (split where it crosses
the extended face's seam and where its curve was computed in two) the
whole run, glued into one edge (JoinRunAcrossSeams), where SelectEdge kept
one piece that stopped short of the edge's end (the slot's round end on
#645's loft without its rounding, top open, Rounded, 1 to 3 mm).
ConcatPCurves moves the second curve by the period where it continues the
first across a seam.

Not covered: #645's rounded loft at 3 mm with the arc join (an exception in
ContextIntByArc); a wall exactly as thick as a circular rounding (see the
topic doc).

The patch's test and its before/after proof:
third_party/vcpkg-ports/docs/opencascade-patch-tests.md, section 2494.

